No it is not.

If that were true, all the file transfer protocol,
secure file transfer protocol, secure hypertext
transfer protocol, streaming audio/video protocols,
MIME protocol, and similar protocols integrated into
your browser would suddenly go missing, and the rich
functionality that users think of as "The Web" would
no longer exist.

The Web has long outgrown its beginnings where http
was the only form of hyperlink in use (and several
of the protocols now integrated into The Web predate
hypertext transfer protocol), and a new hyperlink
protocol is integrated into The Web every year or
two.

There are at least a couple dozen of those hyperlink
protocols which together constitute the session
level of The Web, many no longer in much use, like
gopher: and wais:.

And too, The Web is all seven of the Open Systems
Interconnect Standard levels that make it work, not
just the hyperlink protocols it employs.

