> I'd say RFCs would be a good place.

Gotcha!

The RFCs, in fact this RFC,

http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc1036/rfc1036.html

define the format of a Usenet message; this is what
your just-posted message looks like as the RFCs
define it:

http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/76dbefa86ddd7995?dmode=source

You'll notice that the subject line and the article
body are part of one contiguous body of text, not
separate objects.

If you use a newsreader client that breaks the RFC
format, and in particular breaks the connectedness
of the Usenet news article subject with the Usenet
new article body, you don't get to claim that your
newsreader is "okay because no RFC says not to do
that".

The RFCs define that the two are part of one object.

  The RFCs also don't tell you not to stick the
  keyboard up your nose, that doesn't make doing so
  right, nor give you the right to complain in
  public about what a bad fit it is.

